1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,106
Aarav pulls the plug of a running mixer.

2
00:00:03,106 --> 00:00:05,665
A tiny blue spark jumps at the socket.

3
00:00:06,015 --> 00:00:09,389
Where did a spark strong enough
to jump through air come from?

4
00:00:09,389 --> 00:00:11,097
From a coil inside the motor.

5
00:00:11,597 --> 00:00:14,150
A coil of wire is called an inductor.

6
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:16,228
Its value is called inductance.

7
00:00:16,578 --> 00:00:18,626
Its symbol is a row of loops.

8
00:00:18,626 --> 00:00:22,115
Two straight lines beside
it mean it has an iron core.

9
00:00:22,465 --> 00:00:26,787
Current through a coil makes a magnetic
field, and that field passes through

10
00:00:26,787 --> 00:00:28,340
every turn of the coil.

11
00:00:28,990 --> 00:00:31,566
Let's compare two lamps on the breadboard.

12
00:00:31,566 --> 00:00:33,810
One lamp has a resistor in series.

13
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:35,325
The other has a coil.

14
00:00:35,675 --> 00:00:36,874
Close the switch.

15
00:00:36,874 --> 00:00:38,991
The resistor lamp lights at once.

16
00:00:39,341 --> 00:00:41,503
But the coil lamp brightens slowly.

17
00:00:41,503 --> 00:00:44,663
On the scope, its current
rises in a smooth curve.

18
00:00:45,013 --> 00:00:47,191
The coil is fighting the change.

19
00:00:47,191 --> 00:00:51,594
As the current grows, its magnetic
field grows, and the changing field

20
00:00:51,594 --> 00:00:52,550
pushes back.

21
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:54,671
Now open the switch.

22
00:00:54,671 --> 00:00:57,879
The current tries to drop
to zero in an instant.

23
00:00:57,879 --> 00:00:59,449
The coil fights again.

24
00:00:59,798 --> 00:01:03,384
The scope shows a sharp spike
of voltage across the coil.

25
00:01:03,384 --> 00:01:06,034
That spike is the spark at Aarav's socket.

26
00:01:06,684 --> 00:01:08,575
So how strong is that push back?

27
00:01:08,925 --> 00:01:12,536
The flux through the coil is
proportional to the current.

28
00:01:12,536 --> 00:01:14,809
So N times phi equals L times I.

29
00:01:15,509 --> 00:01:17,604
L is the self inductance.

30
00:01:17,604 --> 00:01:19,594
L equals N phi over I.

31
00:01:20,294 --> 00:01:24,546
By Faraday's law, a changing
flux induces an EMF.

32
00:01:24,546 --> 00:01:28,592
So the EMF equals minus
L times d I by d t.

33
00:01:29,292 --> 00:01:31,539
The minus sign is Lenz's law.

34
00:01:31,539 --> 00:01:35,631
The induced EMF always
opposes the change in current.

35
00:01:36,331 --> 00:01:40,150
EMF in volts, rate of change
in amperes per second.

36
00:01:40,150 --> 00:01:45,220
So L is in volt seconds per ampere,
called the henry, after Joseph Henry.

37
00:01:45,870 --> 00:01:50,738
One henry means one volt is induced,
when the current changes by one ampere

38
00:01:50,738 --> 00:01:51,786
every second.

39
00:01:52,436 --> 00:01:54,898
What decides the inductance of a coil?

40
00:01:55,248 --> 00:01:59,945
For a long coil, L equals mu nought,
times N squared, times A, divided

41
00:01:59,945 --> 00:02:00,845
by l.

42
00:02:01,253 --> 00:02:03,080
N is the number of turns.

43
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:05,065
A is the area of each turn.

44
00:02:05,065 --> 00:02:07,355
And l is the length of the coil.

45
00:02:08,055 --> 00:02:12,888
Mu nought is the permeability of free
space, four pi times ten to the minus

46
00:02:12,888 --> 00:02:14,489
seven henry per metre.

47
00:02:15,189 --> 00:02:20,456
Put an iron core inside, and L is multiplied
by mu r, the relative permeability

48
00:02:20,456 --> 00:02:21,519
of the core.

49
00:02:21,519 --> 00:02:24,122
That can be hundreds of times larger.

50
00:02:24,772 --> 00:02:27,303
That gives us the main types of inductors.

51
00:02:27,653 --> 00:02:32,357
Air core inductors have small inductance,
and suit high frequencies, like

52
00:02:32,357 --> 00:02:33,512
radio circuits.

53
00:02:33,862 --> 00:02:38,731
Iron core inductors, often called
chokes, have large inductance, and suit

54
00:02:38,731 --> 00:02:41,344
low frequencies, like power supplies.

55
00:02:41,694 --> 00:02:44,759
Ferrite core inductors
use a magnetic ceramic.

56
00:02:44,759 --> 00:02:50,115
They pack more inductance into a small
size, and still work at high frequencies.

57
00:02:50,465 --> 00:02:54,848
Variable inductors have a core that
screws in and out, to tune the value.

58
00:02:55,498 --> 00:02:57,632
An inductor does not stop current.

59
00:02:57,632 --> 00:02:59,859
It fights sudden changes in current.

60
00:03:00,659 --> 00:03:01,795
Worked example.

61
00:03:01,795 --> 00:03:04,563
An air core coil has five hundred turns.

62
00:03:04,563 --> 00:03:09,481
It is twenty five centimetres long, and
each turn has an area of four square

63
00:03:09,481 --> 00:03:10,404
centimetres.

64
00:03:10,754 --> 00:03:14,285
N squared is two point
five times ten to the five.

65
00:03:14,285 --> 00:03:17,920
A is four times ten to the
minus four square metres.

66
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:20,540
And l is zero point two five metres.

67
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:26,371
L equals four pi times ten to the
minus seven, times two point five times

68
00:03:26,371 --> 00:03:31,556
ten to the five, times four times ten
to the minus four, over zero point

69
00:03:31,556 --> 00:03:32,456
two five.

70
00:03:33,151 --> 00:03:36,614
That is about five times
ten to the minus four henry.

71
00:03:36,614 --> 00:03:37,907
Half a millihenry.

72
00:03:38,557 --> 00:03:40,023
Now, the exam trap.

73
00:03:40,373 --> 00:03:44,228
Many students think doubling the
turns doubles the inductance.

74
00:03:44,228 --> 00:03:45,248
It does not.

75
00:03:45,598 --> 00:03:47,308
L depends on N squared.

76
00:03:47,308 --> 00:03:52,076
Double the turns, keep the same length
and area, and L becomes four times

77
00:03:52,076 --> 00:03:52,976
larger.

78
00:03:53,390 --> 00:03:55,071
Where is this asked?

79
00:03:55,071 --> 00:04:00,711
JEE Main and NEET ask for L from N
phi equals LI, or from the EMF when

80
00:04:00,711 --> 00:04:02,324
the current changes.

81
00:04:02,674 --> 00:04:07,491
PUC board papers ask you to define
self inductance and the henry, and to

82
00:04:07,491 --> 00:04:09,748
describe the types of inductors.

83
00:04:10,398 --> 00:04:11,298
Try this.

84
00:04:11,294 --> 00:04:16,067
The current in a forty millihenry
coil falls steadily from three amperes

85
00:04:16,067 --> 00:04:18,645
to one ampere in four milliseconds.

86
00:04:18,645 --> 00:04:20,513
What EMF is induced?

87
00:04:25,913 --> 00:04:26,813
B.

88
00:04:26,424 --> 00:04:27,696
Twenty volts.

89
00:04:28,046 --> 00:04:32,699
The current changes by two amperes,
in four times ten to the minus three

90
00:04:32,699 --> 00:04:33,599
seconds.

91
00:04:33,768 --> 00:04:37,469
So d I by d t is five
hundred amperes per second.

92
00:04:37,819 --> 00:04:40,806
The EMF is L times d I by d t.

93
00:04:40,806 --> 00:04:45,049
Zero point zero four times
five hundred is twenty volts.

94
00:04:45,699 --> 00:04:47,223
Screenshot this card.

95
00:04:50,423 --> 00:04:53,192
Next, energy storage
in passive components.

96
00:04:53,192 --> 00:04:56,988
Where does the energy hide,
in a capacitor and in a coil?
